Communal harmony: disagreement, cooperation, and application (@buddhist-communal-harmony: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-communal-harmony, #philosophy @buddhist-core-completion, #buddhism @buddhist-core-completion) { r_1BKX3CXYEZ43X3E40A5J7QERN6
**Read:** the introduction and complete [The Buddha’s Teachings on Social and Communal Harmony](../sources/Communal-harmony.lingua#r_012ATA9STCZ0B54TRNNF427RT5), following the anthology’s order. Keep its original discourse identifiers and revisit relevant passages in the collections already read.

Ethics has been present since the first book. Here investigate community, generosity, disagreement, compassion, and cooperation as connected concerns. Distinguish an ancient monastic setting, an instruction for lay people, the anthology’s arrangement, and a present social proposal.

**Develop an application:** choose a concrete disagreement in a community or institution. Reconstruct one relevant teaching, explain what it illuminates, and name an important feature of the modern case it does not address. Compare an objection to your proposal and revise its scope accordingly.

**Check:** identify when an implication follows from the cited teaching and when it depends on an additional ethical or empirical premise. This completes the eighth book; continue with the connected account of the core before choosing later readings.

**Continue:** [Buddhism: completing the core reading](../research/Core-completion.lingua#r_562HHAD8ARXMYG8DC0GP1QKYAT).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
